 questions: [
  ["Which irrigant is the only one in routine use that dissolves necrotic and vital pulp tissue?","Sodium hypochlorite",["Chlorhexidine gluconate","EDTA","Sterile saline"],"NaOCl breaks down organic tissue as well as killing bacteria. Chlorhexidine is antibacterial but does not dissolve tissue, and EDTA acts on mineral."],
  ["A final rinse of 17% EDTA is used after instrumentation to:","Remove the inorganic part of the smear layer by chelating calcium",["Dissolve remaining pulp tissue","Neutralise residual sodium hypochlorite","Provide prolonged antibacterial action in dentine"],"Filing leaves a smear layer of crushed dentine and debris; EDTA chelates its mineral part and opens the tubules. Tissue dissolution is NaOCl's job, and prolonged binding to dentine describes chlorhexidine."],
  ["A brown precipitate forms in the canal when chlorhexidine is introduced after sodium hypochlorite. The most appropriate way to prevent this is to:","Rinse with saline between the two irrigants",["Use a higher concentration of chlorhexidine","Warm the hypochlorite before use","Use EDTA and chlorhexidine together as a mixture"],"The two irrigants react on contact; an intermediate flush removes residual NaOCl. Changing concentrations or temperatures does not stop the reaction."],
  ["A size 30 hand file with a standard 0.02 taper has a working part 16 mm long. Its diameter at the end of the working part is:","0.62 mm",["0.32 mm","0.46 mm","0.94 mm"],"The tip is 0.30 mm and the diameter grows by 0.02 mm per mm: 0.30 + (16 x 0.02) = 0.62 mm. Choosing 0.32 mm adds only the taper increase and forgets the tip."],
  ["A rotary NiTi file separates without visible unwinding in the curved mesiobuccal canal of tooth 4.6. The most likely mechanism is:","Cyclic fatigue from repeated bending while rotating in the curve",["Torsional failure from the tip binding in the canal","Corrosion by sodium hypochlorite","Excessive flexibility of the alloy"],"Each rotation in a curve bends the metal back and forth until it cracks, often with no visible warning. Torsional failure usually shows unwinding or distortion first."],
  ["Which practice best reduces the risk of forcing sodium hypochlorite beyond the apex?","A side-vented needle that stays loose in the canal, short of working length, with gentle pressure",["An open-ended needle wedged at working length for better flushing","Irrigating with high pressure to clear debris quickly","Omitting rubber dam so excess irrigant can escape"],"Extrusion happens when the needle binds and pressure drives irrigant through the foramen. A loose, side-vented needle short of WL lets fluid flow back coronally. Rubber dam protects the mouth and airway."],
  ["Calcium hydroxide is placed in a canal between appointments. Its antibacterial action is mainly due to:","Its high pH",["Release of free chlorine","Chelation of calcium from dentine","Coagulation of bacterial proteins by formaldehyde"],"Hydroxyl ions raise the pH to a level most canal bacteria cannot survive. E. faecalis tolerates high pH, which is one reason it is often recovered from persistent infections."],
  ["Which of the following best describes the role of Enterococcus faecalis in failed root canal treatment?","It is frequently recovered and is one contributor alongside missed canals and leakage",["It is the single cause of all endodontic failures","It is never found in root-filled teeth","It causes failure only when the root filling is overextended"],"E. faecalis survives high pH and starvation and is often isolated from root-filled teeth with persistent lesions. Failure is multifactorial: missed anatomy, poor cleaning and coronal leakage matter at least as much."],
  ["Compared with stainless steel, nickel-titanium files are preferred in curved canals mainly because they:","Are superelastic and follow the canal curve with less transportation",["Cut dentine faster in straight canals","Never fracture during use","Can be precurved permanently to match the canal"],"NiTi flexibility keeps the file centred in a curve and reduces ledges and zips. NiTi can still fracture, and it cannot be precurved the way steel can."],
  ["The primary role of the sealer in an obturated canal is to:","Fill the spaces between gutta-percha cones and between cones and the canal wall",["Provide the bulk of the root filling","Dissolve remaining tissue in lateral canals","Bond the root filling to the crown restoration"],"Gutta-percha forms the core but cannot adapt to every irregularity; sealer fills the gaps and voids. Sealer is a thin layer and does not dissolve tissue."],
  ["A root filling in tooth 3.6 (necrotic pulp with apical periodontitis) ends 3 mm short of the radiographic apex. Which of the following best explains the poorer prognosis?","Infected, uncleaned canal remains apical to the filling",["The gutta-percha will dissolve over time","The sealer will extrude into the PDL","The crown will be too weak to restore"],"In a necrotic tooth the apical canal is infected; a short preparation and filling leave bacteria behind to sustain the lesion. Sealer extrusion is a problem of overfilling, not underfilling."],
  ["A well-filled root-treated tooth 2.6 lost its temporary restoration three months ago and was left open to the mouth. The most appropriate next step before a crown is to:","Consider retreatment, because saliva may have recontaminated the canal",["Place the crown directly over the existing root filling","Prescribe antibiotics to sterilise the canal","Apply fluoride varnish to the access cavity"],"Bacteria from saliva can travel the length of a root filling within weeks once the coronal seal is lost, so coronal leakage is a recognised cause of failure. Antibiotics cannot reach a canal without a blood supply."],
  ["A ledge forms in the apical third of the curved mesiobuccal canal of tooth 1.6. The most likely cause is:","Forcing an uncurved stainless steel file of increasing size in the curve",["Using EDTA as a final rinse","Crown-down preparation with NiTi rotary files","Irrigating with 1% rather than 5% sodium hypochlorite"],"Stiff steel files try to straighten and dig into the outer wall of a curve, cutting a ledge. Precurving, small sizes, a glide path and NiTi reduce the risk; irrigant choice does not cause ledges."]
 ]
