 teach: {
  prompt: "A parent phones from a football pitch: their 14-year-old's front tooth has been knocked out. Talk them through what to do in the next ten minutes, and explain why each step matters.",
  model: "Find the tooth and pick it up by the white crown, not the root, because the root is covered in living ligament cells that heal it back in. If it is dirty, rinse it briefly in milk, saline or the child's saliva, then push it gently back into the socket the right way round, and have them bite on a cloth to hold it. Putting it back within minutes gives the best chance of the ligament surviving. If you cannot replant it, put it straight into milk, or have the child keep it in saliva, because cells start dying within minutes of drying and most are dead after half an hour dry. Water is poor but better than leaving it dry. Then come to a dentist immediately for a splint and antibiotics, and bring the tetanus record."
 },
