 questions: [
  ["A parent phones 5 minutes after their 12-year-old's tooth 1.1 was avulsed. They cannot replant it. The most appropriate storage advice is to place the tooth in:","Milk",["Tap water","A dry tissue","A bag of ice"],"IADT 2020 ranks milk first among readily available media because it is near-isotonic and keeps PDL cells alive. Water is hypotonic and only better than drying, and a dry tissue or ice damages the cells."],
  ["According to IADT 2020, most periodontal ligament cells on an avulsed tooth are non-viable after an extra-oral dry time of:","30 minutes",["5 minutes","2 hours","24 hours"],"Dehydration kills PDL cells within minutes, and after 30 minutes dry most are non-viable; over 60 minutes dry the PDL is treated as non-viable whatever the storage. This is why immediate replantation is stressed."],
  ["A replanted avulsed tooth 2.1 with no alveolar fracture should be stabilised with:","A passive flexible splint for 2 weeks",["A rigid splint for 4 weeks","A passive flexible splint for 6 weeks","No splint, relying on the soft tissues"],"Slight physiological movement promotes PDL healing and lowers ankylosis risk. A more rigid splint for about 4 weeks is reserved for associated alveolar or jaw fractures."],
  ["Tooth 1.1, with a closed apex, was replanted after avulsion in a 16-year-old. Root canal treatment should be started:","Within 2 weeks of replantation",["Only if the tooth fails to respond to cold at 3 months","At the emergency visit, before replantation, in every case","After the 1-year review"],"A mature avulsed tooth will not revascularise, and its infected necrotic pulp drives fast inflammatory resorption, so IADT 2020 starts treatment within 2 weeks. Waiting for test results risks losing the root."],
  ["An 8-year-old has had tooth 1.1 replanted after avulsion. Which systemic antibiotic is most appropriate?","Amoxicillin",["Doxycycline","Tetracycline","Metronidazole alone"],"IADT 2020 names amoxicillin or penicillin as first choices. Tetracyclines, including doxycycline, are generally avoided under 12 because they can discolour developing permanent teeth."],
  ["After replanting an avulsed tooth contaminated with soil, the clinician should also:","Check tetanus status and refer to a physician if in doubt",["Give a tetanus booster in the dental office in every case","Ignore tetanus because the tooth was replanted","Delay replantation until tetanus status is confirmed"],"IADT advises checking tetanus status and referring for medical assessment of the need for a booster. Replantation must not be delayed for this."],
  ["A 15-year-old has a horizontal mid-root fracture of tooth 2.1 with a mobile, slightly displaced coronal segment. The most appropriate management is:","Reposition the coronal segment and apply a passive flexible splint for 4 weeks",["Extract the coronal segment and leave the apical segment","Start root canal treatment of both segments at the emergency visit","Apply a rigid splint for 12 weeks"],"IADT 2020: reposition and splint flexibly for 4 weeks (up to 4 months for cervical fractures). Pulps often survive, and if necrosis follows it usually affects only the coronal segment, so emergency root canal treatment is not done."],
  ["Tooth 1.1 in a 20-year-old is displaced palatally, firmly locked and gives a high metallic note on percussion. The radiograph shows a widened PDL space. The most appropriate splinting is:","A passive flexible splint for 4 weeks after repositioning",["A passive flexible splint for 2 weeks after repositioning","No splint, because the tooth is immobile","A rigid splint for 8 weeks"],"These are signs of lateral luxation: the apex is locked in bone and must be freed before repositioning, and IADT recommends a 4-week flexible splint. Two weeks is the extrusion and avulsion interval."],
  ["An 8-year-old has intruded tooth 1.1 by 4 mm. The apex is wide open. The most appropriate initial management is:","Allow spontaneous re-eruption and monitor",["Immediate surgical repositioning and splinting","Extraction of the intruded tooth","Immediate root canal treatment"],"IADT 2020 lets immature intruded teeth re-erupt whatever the degree, and starts orthodontic repositioning if there is no re-eruption within 8 weeks. Immediate endodontics would sacrifice a pulp that may revascularise."],
  ["A 25-year-old has intruded tooth 2.1 by 8 mm. The apex is closed. The most appropriate management is:","Surgical repositioning, a flexible splint for 4 weeks, and root canal treatment starting at about 2 weeks",["Allow spontaneous re-eruption and review at 8 weeks","Extraction and immediate implant","Orthodontic extrusion starting after 6 months"],"Mature teeth intruded more than 7 mm are repositioned surgically, and their pulps almost always become necrotic, so endodontic treatment starts at about 2 weeks. Spontaneous re-eruption is expected only in immature teeth or minor mature intrusions."],
  ["A 13-year-old has a concussion injury to tooth 1.2: tender to percussion, not mobile, not displaced, with a normal radiograph. The most appropriate management is:","No splint; review clinically and radiographically at 4 weeks and 1 year",["A flexible splint for 2 weeks","Root canal treatment within 2 weeks","Occlusal reduction and a 4-week splint"],"Concussion injures the PDL without loosening the tooth, so IADT recommends no treatment, only monitoring of the pulp. A splint is not needed when the tooth is not mobile."],
  ["Six weeks after a subluxation injury to tooth 1.1 (closed apex), cold testing still gives no response, but there are no symptoms, no discoloration and no radiographic change. The most appropriate decision is:","Continue monitoring, because loss of response after luxation is often temporary",["Start root canal treatment because the pulp is necrotic","Extract the tooth because the PDL has failed","Prescribe antibiotics to protect the pulp"],"IADT 2020 warns that sensibility can be lost temporarily for months after luxation, and endodontic treatment should not start on that alone. Treat when other signs of necrosis and infection appear."],
  ["Two years after replantation, tooth 2.1 in a 12-year-old is 2 mm short of the occlusal plane, gives a high metallic percussion note, and on the radiograph the PDL space has disappeared and root is being replaced by bone. The most likely diagnosis is:","Ankylosis-related (replacement) resorption",["Infection-related (inflammatory) resorption","Internal root resorption","Pulp canal obliteration"],"Fusion of root to bone removes the PDL space and gives a metallic note; the tooth stops erupting while the jaw grows, so it becomes infrapositioned. Inflammatory resorption shows radiolucent defects in root and bone instead."],
  ["An 8-year-old has a complicated crown fracture of tooth 1.1 with a 1 mm pulp exposure 4 hours old. The apex is open. The most appropriate treatment is:","Partial pulpotomy with a calcium silicate cement, then restoration",["Pulpectomy and gutta-percha obturation","Calcium hydroxide apexification","Extraction and a space maintainer"],"Keeping the pulp alive lets the root keep growing; partial pulpotomy is the IADT-preferred treatment in immature teeth. Pulpectomy would stop root development and leave a thin, short root."]
 ]
