 questions: [
  ["The most common cause of persistent apical periodontitis after root canal treatment is:","Bacteria persisting in or re-entering the root canal system",["A true radicular cyst","A foreign-body reaction to gutta-percha","Actinomyces infection outside the root"],"Intraradicular infection from missed canals, poor cleaning or coronal leakage explains most failures. Cysts, foreign-body reactions and extraradicular actinomycosis are real but less common."],
  ["Which statement best describes the role of Enterococcus faecalis in failed root canal treatment?","It is often recovered from such teeth and is one of several contributors",["It is the sole cause of every failed root canal treatment","It cannot survive in a root-filled canal","It is eliminated reliably by calcium hydroxide"],"E. faecalis tolerates high pH and nutrient deprivation and is often isolated from failed cases. Failure is multifactorial, and calcium hydroxide does not reliably eliminate this organism."],
  ["A 47-year-old has a growing apical lesion at tooth 2.5, which was root filled 4 years ago. The root filling ends 2 mm short, has voids, and the composite restoration is leaking. The most appropriate treatment is:","Non-surgical retreatment and a new coronal seal",["Apicoectomy with a root-end filling","A course of amoxicillin","Extraction and an implant"],"A deficient filling and coronal leakage place the cause inside the canal, which retreatment reaches and surgery does not. Extraction is premature for a treatable, restorable tooth."],
  ["A 55-year-old has a persistent lesion at tooth 1.2, which has a well-condensed root filling to the correct length, a long cast post and a crown with sound margins. The most appropriate treatment is:","Apical surgery with a root-end filling",["Non-surgical retreatment after removing the post","Antibiotics and review in 3 months","Observation without treatment"],"When the root filling is already good and removing the post risks fracturing the root, surgery addresses the apical cause directly. Observation is not appropriate for a lesion that has persisted."],
  ["In modern apical microsurgery, about 3 mm of the root end is resected because this:","Removes most apical ramifications and lateral canals",["Always shortens the root to the level of the lesion","Exposes the most dentinal tubules for the root-end filling","Removes all cementum from the apical third"],"Most apical branches lie within the last 3 mm of the root, so resecting this much removes them while preserving root length. Exposing more tubules is a disadvantage, not an aim."],
  ["The root-end cavity in modern apical surgery is best prepared:","With ultrasonic tips to about 3 mm along the long axis of the root",["With a round bur on a steep bevel","With a fissure bur perpendicular to the root surface","By acid etching without mechanical preparation"],"Ultrasonic tips cut a small, deep cavity that follows the canal and lets the cut be made with little bevel. Bur preparations on a steep bevel open more tubules and can perforate the root."],
  ["The most appropriate root-end filling material in modern apical surgery is:","A calcium silicate cement such as MTA",["Amalgam","Gutta-percha alone","Zinc phosphate cement"],"Calcium silicate cements seal well in moist conditions and are biocompatible, supporting cementum and bone repair over them. Amalgam was used historically but leaks and corrodes."],
  ["A minimal bevel on the resected root end is preferred mainly because it:","Exposes fewer dentinal tubules and is less likely to miss lingual canal anatomy",["Shortens the root more than a steep bevel","Allows amalgam to be condensed more easily","Removes the need for a root-end filling"],"A steep bevel cuts across many tubules that can leak, and its long oval face can leave lingual canals unseen. Ultrasonic preparation made the minimal bevel practical."],
  ["Tooth 3.6 in a 38-year-old does not respond to cold. There is a narrow 8 mm probing defect at the mid-buccal site, while all other sites probe 2-3 mm, and a sinus tract drains through the sulcus. The most appropriate initial treatment is:","Root canal treatment, then reassess before any periodontal surgery",["Periodontal flap surgery with bone grafting","Scaling and root planing of the defect only","Extraction of tooth 3.6"],"A necrotic pulp with a narrow isolated defect draining through the sulcus suggests a primary endodontic lesion, which usually heals after root canal treatment alone. Periodontal surgery would be unnecessary and harmful."],
  ["Tooth 4.7 in a 60-year-old has generalised 6-7 mm pockets with calculus, as do the neighbouring teeth, and responds normally to cold. The most appropriate treatment for tooth 4.7 is:","Periodontal therapy without root canal treatment",["Root canal treatment followed by periodontal therapy","Root canal treatment alone","Apical surgery"],"A responsive pulp and widespread pockets with calculus make this a primary periodontal problem. Root canal treatment of a healthy pulp would add nothing."],
  ["A tooth has a necrotic pulp and deep periodontal pockets from established periodontitis. The usual sequence of treatment is:","Root canal treatment first, then reassessment and periodontal therapy",["Periodontal surgery first, then root canal treatment","Both completed at the same appointment","Periodontal therapy alone, monitoring the pulp"],"Removing the endodontic infection first shows how much of the defect was pulpal; the remaining periodontal component is then treated. The periodontal part usually decides the prognosis."],
  ["Apical surgery is planned for tooth 3.5. The structure most at risk is the:","Mental nerve",["Inferior alveolar nerve at the mandibular foramen","Lingual nerve","Maxillary sinus"],"The mental foramen usually lies near the apices of the mandibular premolars, so the flap and osteotomy must protect the mental nerve. The mandibular foramen is on the medial ramus, far from this site."],
  ["Post-treatment healing of a periapical lesion after root canal treatment is best assessed:","Clinically and radiographically over at least one year",["Only by the absence of pain at one week","By pulp sensibility testing of the treated tooth","By a single radiograph taken at obturation"],"Bone takes time to refill, so lesions are followed for at least a year and sometimes up to four. Absence of pain alone is not proof of healing, and a root-filled tooth cannot respond to sensibility tests."]
 ]
